## Getting Started

### Setup

```bash
# Clone and build
git clone https://github.com/inputlayer/inputlayer.git
cd inputlayer
cargo build

# Run tests (do this frequently!)
cargo test
./scripts/run_snapshot_tests.sh

# Check code quality
cargo clippy -- -D warnings
cargo fmt --check
```

### Pre-commit Checks

Optional git hooks run the fast checks CI also runs, with the same commands:

```bash
make install-hooks                  # once per clone; make uninstall-hooks removes them
make install-gitleaks               # pinned scanner in target/tools/, if gitleaks is not on PATH
```

| Hook | Runs | CI job |
|------|------|--------|
| pre-commit | `make fmt-check` (only when `.rs` files are staged), then `scripts/secret-scan.sh --staged` | Format, Secret scan |
| pre-push | `make lint` | Clippy |

`core.hooksPath` is per clone, so the hooks cover every worktree of it; the
installer refuses to replace a hooks path you already use. Bypass a hook once
with `--no-verify`. If the secret scan fires, remove the credential and rotate
it if it ever left your machine; a documentation placeholder that trips the
scan goes into `.gitleaks.toml` as an exact value, never as a path or rule
exemption. `make secret-check` scans every commit reachable from HEAD, and
`make hooks-test` proves the hooks reject misformatted Rust and a staged
credential. ESLint for the GUI is not hooked; it belongs to the deferred UI work.

Hooks never run tests. Before each commit, run the tests that cover your
change: `make test-fast` (or the touched crate's `cargo test -p ...`) and the
affected snapshot specs (`make test-affected`).

Before opening a PR, `make pre-pr` must pass. It runs formatting, clippy and
the tests that apply (workspace tests plus the snapshot categories affected
since your branch left `main`) in parallel, then the performance gate. Attach
the gate's report (`target/perf-gate/latest/report.md`) to the PR:

```bash
make pre-pr                         # fast checks in parallel, then make perf-gate
make perf-gate                      # the gate alone; see perf-gate/README.md
```

Changes to subscriptions, notifications or the WebSocket protocol also run
`make e2e-reactive` (agents subscribed to a real engine; see `TESTING.md`).

---

## Key Principles (Must Read)

These principles exist to prevent the issues we're currently fixing. Please follow them:

### 1. Single Source of Truth

**One definition per concept. Re-export, don't duplicate.**

```rust
// WRONG: Defining same type in multiple places
// ast/mod.rs:  enum AggregateFunc { ... }
// ir/mod.rs:   enum AggregateFunction { ... }  // Different name!
// statement.rs: enum SerializableAggregateFunc { ... }  // Third copy!

// RIGHT: Single definition, re-exported
// types/aggregates.rs:
pub enum Aggregate { ... }

// Other modules re-export:
pub use crate::types::Aggregate;
```

### 2. No Legacy Compatibility Code

**We have no users. Delete old code, don't maintain it.**

```rust
// WRONG: Keeping legacy code paths
type OldTuple = (i32, i32);         // Old format
fn execute_old() -> Vec<OldTuple>   // "Just in case"
fn execute_new() -> Vec<Tuple>      // The real one

// RIGHT: One way to do things (Tuple is the only format now)
fn execute() -> Vec<Tuple>
```

### 3. Types Flow Through the Pipeline

**Type information should be available at every stage.**

```rust
// WRONG: Losing type info in IR
Scan { relation: String, schema: Vec<String> }  // Only names!

// RIGHT: Preserve types
Scan { relation: String, schema: TupleSchema }  // Full type info
```

### 4. Small, Focused Modules

**Max 500 lines per file. Single responsibility.**

```rust
// WRONG: 2,860-line monolith
// statement.rs handles: parsing, serialization, conversion, meta commands...

// RIGHT: Focused modules
// statement/parser.rs   - Core parsing
// statement/meta.rs     - Meta commands
// statement/serialize.rs - JSON serialization
```

### 5. Helpful Error Messages

**Tell users what went wrong AND how to fix it.**

```rust
// WRONG
Err("parse error")

// RIGHT
Err(format!(
    "Unknown relation '{}' at line {}. \
     Available relations: {}",
    name, line, available.join(", ")
))
```

